From 67b5da3849b11040348f564fcd2e70dd321c2657 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 04:19:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] A processor that cannot decode both
 views in time is found by the decoder's work, in four seconds

Where the processor decodes both views of a 3D stream and does not keep
up, the stream goes back to its base view in 2D. That was decided by
the playback falling behind: two windows of four seconds in a row with
one picture in eight late, after a grace of two seconds, so ten seconds
and more of a picture that juddered first (a Radeon 780M laptop with a
4K MV-HEVC film: 15 to 20 seconds, each time it was played).

The decoder's own work tells it sooner. The player now measures the
time it spends in the decoder of both views (sending it packets, asking
it for pictures) between one picture of two views and the next. With
threads, the decoder takes a packet only when one of them is free, so
where the processor is what holds the playback back this time is what a
picture costs; where it keeps up, the player waits elsewhere and the
time is short. After the first 8 pictures of a start or a seek (the
threads fill), where two windows of 48 pictures in a row give fewer
pictures a second of that work than nine tenths of the video's rate,
the stream goes back to its base view at once: about four seconds into
the film. A processor that keeps up, or nearly, is left to the rule of
the playback, which stays as it was; NVDEC is not measured this way.

kodi.log says the rate measured and the video's: "the processor does
not decode both views in time: it gives N pictures of two views a
second of its work, measured over two windows of 48 pictures in a row,
and the video has R", and the player information says that the second
view was given up because the processor does not keep up, as before.
